rows.en:
...Haven't you ever heard it?
The story of a child who died at this school.
It was a popular topic in my class back when I was a first-year.{p}

For our classmate Nao, it must have been complicated.
A boy with the same name as his was the talk of the class, after all.
Well, Nao was popular, so nobody bullied him over it or anything like that.{p}

Then on Monday, friends would trade information: which places they'd searched, whether they'd found anything like a clue, "I saw something like a child's ghost," that sort of thing.
A stupid game.
Quite a lot of them exaggerated, or twisted things to fit, too.{p}

...As for places to hunt for the dead child, the old school building was the most popular.
It's off-limits to begin with, isn't it?
Naturally, that makes it suspicious.{p}

The year head came to our class again and again.
He was a rugged, fairly strict teacher, so the students usually did what he said.
But on this matter, he seemed completely at his wits' end.{p}

Heh heh...
I thought so.
But I didn't tell anyone about it.{p}

After all, I'm no detective.
Whether Nao was strange or not didn't matter to me.
I don't care whether my classmates get hurt or die, either.{p}

A small child's ghost was sitting on the teacher's shoulders.
As if he were giving it a shoulder ride.
The ghost wrapped its legs around his neck.{p}

The teacher's scream, and the sound of bones snapping.
The teacher, who had been glaring with his eyes rolling wildly, went still that very instant.
His arms and legs dangled limp, like a puppet's.{p}

Oh, is that what you're curious about?
Heh heh... Don't worry, I'll tell you.
But first, Nao's side of the story.{p}

Did you see a child's body?
If you say you did, the child's ghost will get you and you'll be shut in next to the body, he told him.
Nao said he hadn't seen anything like that.{p}

I see...
It's a rather nasty story.
I can't really say whether something like this should go in your article.{p}

Nao said that when the year head came to our class to warn us, he thought something was off about him.
So he looked into it in all sorts of ways.
What I'm about to tell you is partly his own guesswork.{p}

With her child gone, she was devastated, and the year head whispered sweet words to her.
You can talk to me about anything, I'll be your support... He kept telling her whatever suited him.
...After days like that, she finally went and married the year head.{p}

The murdered child's ghost was looking for its mommy, remember?
And that mommy had married the man who killed it.
Nao just couldn't bring himself to tell it something like that.{p}

...Even so, I feel sick.
Isn't there something in this room?
The child's ghost? Or perhaps...{p}

Well, never mind. Whatever is in here, the one who gets cursed will be whoever is in charge of this gathering.
My condolences, {a}.{p}Well then, that's the end of my story.
Whose turn is it next?{p}

...Well, fine.
The seventh one doesn't look likely to show up anyway, and once I've told mine, there will only be two left.
Let's get this over with quickly.{p}

Oh, please. Don't look at me with such frightened eyes.
It makes me want to kill you.
When people cower like that, it only makes me angrier.{p}

Either you don't know what passing on means, or you're remarkably naive.
Either way, I'd rather not have much to do with foolish people like that.
If it were me, there's no way I could ever pass on.{p}

"Mister, come find me."
The child stayed riding on Nao's shoulders the whole time.
No matter how fast he ran, he couldn't shake it off.{p}

Nao was shut inside that same classroom once again.
Would this go on forever?
As he sank into despair, the child started saying this.{p}

...Nao couldn't get out of that classroom in the old school building, again.
This time it was even more miserable.
He had fallen into the hole in the floor, so his chances of being found were even slimmer.{p}

He spent that night inside the hole.
What will I do if I die like this?
An indescribable dread took hold of him.{p}

Then the boy grabbed Nao by the hair as hard as it could.
"I know. How about I make sure you can never run away again?"
And with that, his fate was sealed.{p}

...The next day, Nao was gone from his hospital room.
The nurses and the doctors searched for him, but in the end, he was never found.
Can you guess where he went?{p}

"I'm sick, you know."
That's what Nao said.
And then the child said something like this to him.{p}

Well, a place like that might be suspicious too.
But I don't think you could call it the most suspicious.
After all, the students had all been searching mainly around the old school building.{p}
